# Build all SGAI artifacts (orchestrator)

**Inputs**: `context/` (canonical, human-authored spec files). Per-step
inputs are declared in each individual prompt's header — this orchestrator
does not duplicate them.

**Outputs** (per major iteration `v<N>` and optional minor iterations
`v<N>.<M>` from Step 9):
- `context-analysis/` — pre-spec generated artefacts (5 files, one per
  Step 1-5 prompt). See `1-pre-spec/*.prompt` for individual contracts.
- `spec-source/` — the source of the spec (ADR 0060), edited by patches
  (ADR 0066). **Invariant: `spec-source/` is always the source of the
  newest candidate in `output/`** (`bin/assemble-spec.py --check` on it
  exits 0); every step that edits it either assembles a new candidate or
  undoes its edit.
- `output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md` — the spec, assembled from `spec-source/`
  after the major delta (Step 6, `2-build/apply-context-delta.prompt` in
  major mode), and `output/v<N>-sgai-extension.xsd`, its extension schema
  (Step 6.1). The patch records, the source manifest and the trace
  (`output-analysis/v<N>-delta-patch-<k>.json`, `v<N>-source.sha256`,
  `v<N>-context-delta.md`) go with it.
- `output-analysis/v<N>-*` — per major iteration: the conformance tests
  (Step 8.0, a `.md` and a `.json`), the validation with its trace and
  summary (Step 7), the detail review and its patch (Step 7.5) and the
  audit's suspicions (Step 8); see `3-post-spec/`. Auto-refine iterations
  (Step 9) produce their patch, the candidate and its schema, the same
  sidecars except the audit (carried, ADR 0071), and a
  `vN.M-comparison.md` (see `4-auto-refine/`).

Every output above is a **candidate**. This orchestrator never writes
to `dist/`, which holds the published build and receives a candidate
only by promotion — a decision outside this pipeline.
- Versioning: `N` = major (computed once per build-all invocation),
  `M` = minor (incremented by Step 9 for each accepted refinement).
  `N` is computed as `max(existing v*-sgai-spec.md in output/) + 1`
  (parse the integer after the leading `v` and BEFORE any `.` in
  each filename). If no prior build exists, `N` starts at 1. `M`
  starts at 0 (the major output is `v<N>-...`, treated as `M=0` for
  the arithmetic) and is incremented by Step 9 for each accepted
  refinement.

**Skip-if**: each prompt declares its own skip rule and is honoured here.
Exceptions: Step 5 (`analyze-iab-ad-templates`) NEVER skips (external live
source). Step 9 (auto-refine) is gated by `BUILD_ALL_AUTO_REFINE` env
var (default `true`) and capped at `MAX_REFINEMENTS` iterations
(default `5`); see Step 9 configuration below for details.

---

## Step 0 — Confirm the normative base (blocking, runs first)

Before any other step, run from the project root:

```bash
bin/check-normative-base.py
```

**A non-zero exit aborts the build.** Do not run Step 1, do not fall
back, do not reason about whether the mismatch matters — report the
script's output verbatim and stop.

The script compares `context/00-normative-base.md` against the cover of
the primary copy of the standard on disk. It fails when the declared
edition does not describe that document, when the file at the declared
path is no longer the declared file, and when it cannot read the
document at all. That last case is an error and not a skip: a check that
passes because it could not look is worse than no check.

A warning about revalidation age does not abort the build. Surface it.

## Step 0.5 — Confirm `context/` is internally consistent (blocking)

After Step 0 and before any step that produces anything, run:

```bash
bin/check-context-coherence.py
```

**A non-zero exit aborts the build**, for the same reason as Step 0:
nothing should be generated from an input that does not hold together.
It reports dangling references to requirements, criteria, design
principles, extension rules, out-of-scope items and use cases,
identifiers defined twice, and broken relative links.

It checks that the identifiers line up and **nothing about whether the
content is right**. A contradiction between a requirement and its own
criteria, a gist that describes half of its body, or a citation
pointing at the neighbouring clause all pass this step. Do not report
its green as "`context/` is fine".

## Step 0.6 — Confirm `context/` holds its vocabulary (blocking)

After Step 0.5 and before any step that produces anything, run:

```bash
bin/check-context-vocabulary.py
```

**A non-zero exit aborts the build**, for the same reason as Step 0.5.
Exit `2` — cannot tell — aborts as well: it means an anchor the check
reads its rules from is missing, so zero findings would say nothing.

Same files, the other axis. It reports values used outside the closed
enumeration that declares them, enumerated items with no token to write
them with, mentions of a term a requirement confines to illustrative
material, RFC 2119 modals in constructions that bind no one, a file
that calls itself normative and obliges no one, frequent multi-word
terms with no glossary entry, and open questions missing from the
register.

Every one of those is a defect that reaches the generated spec as
vocabulary the build has to invent or as an obligation it cannot place.
Its green says the vocabulary closes and the modals are well formed; it
says nothing about whether a definition is right or whether two
requirements contradict each other. Do not report it as "`context/` is
fine" either.

## Step 0.7 — Confirm `context/03-requirements.md` keeps its interfaces (blocking)

After Step 0.6 and before any step that produces anything, run:

```bash
bin/check-context-interfaces.py
```

**A non-zero exit aborts the build**, for the same reason as Step 0.5;
exit `2` — cannot tell — aborts as well. It reports index rows of the
"Interfaces" section that point to a criterion that does not exist or that
the party writing that interface does not hold, an element indexed twice,
a retired identifier cited outside the "Retired identifiers" table, and a
criterion that vanished without a row there.

Its green says the index and the identifiers hold together. It says
nothing about whether a meaning is written once: a criterion that
restates another in other words, or a Player criterion that follows from
the document, passes this step.

## Pipeline

For each step, log a single line:
`[BUILT|SKIPPED] <step> → <output>`.

Each downstream prompt also logs its own
`[GROUNDED_BY=iso-23009-1-2026-pdf]` or `[GROUNDED_BY=spec-only]` line when it
runs (skipped steps emit no grounding line). Surface those lines as
they appear so the full run is auditable.

The orchestrator runs the following stages in order. Each stage maps
1:1 to a subfolder of `prompts/`.

### Stage 1 — Pre-spec (`1-pre-spec/`)

Generate the inputs that the spec build consumes.

#### Step 1 — Gap analysis

Run `python3 bin/step-inputs.py check analyze-dash-gap` (it compares the
content of the step's inputs with the hashes recorded when its output was
built; a date never decides):

- exit 0 → log
  `[SKIPPED] analyze-dash-gap → context-analysis/dash-gap-analysis.md`
  and continue.
- exit 1 or 2 → invoke the instructions in
  `prompts/1-pre-spec/analyze-dash-gap.prompt`, then run
  `python3 bin/step-inputs.py record analyze-dash-gap`, and log
  `[BUILT] analyze-dash-gap → context-analysis/dash-gap-analysis.md`.

#### Step 3 — Error semantics matrix

Same rule with the step `build-error-semantics`: `check` exit 0 skips;
otherwise invoke `prompts/1-pre-spec/build-error-semantics.prompt`, then
`record`, and log
`[BUILT] build-error-semantics → context-analysis/error-semantics.md`.

Steps 2 (UC coverage matrix) and 4 (conformance assertions) are retired
(ADR 0072): no step read their output. The UC coverage comes from the
scenario vectors and the assertions from the conformance tests' levels 1
and 2. Their files in `context-analysis/` stay as a record and are not
regenerated.

#### Step 5 — IAB ad-template catalogue (live mirror)

This step regenerates `context-analysis/iab-ad-templates.md` from the live
IAB ad-template catalogue (external Google Doc; see
`prompts/1-pre-spec/analyze-iab-ad-templates.prompt` for the source link).
**This step NEVER skips by mtime** — the source is a live external
document that updates independently. Always invoke the prompt; the
output file is overwritten in place.

- Always invoke the instructions in
  `prompts/1-pre-spec/analyze-iab-ad-templates.prompt` and log
  `[BUILT] analyze-iab-ad-templates → context-analysis/iab-ad-templates.md`.
  If the fetch fails inside the prompt (paywall, network, missing
  tool), the prompt produces a file documenting the failure with an
  empty table — that is NOT a build error, the pipeline continues.
  Surface the prompt's `[GROUNDED_BY=iab-live-link]` or
  `[GROUNDED_BY=spec-only]` log line so the run is auditable.

### Stage 2 — Build (`2-build/`)

#### Step 6 — Major delta on the source

A major is no longer a document generated from scratch (ADR 0067): it is
`prompts/2-build/apply-context-delta.prompt` in **major mode** — the same
path as an incremental, with no changed-fraction cap and allowed to add
and retire constructs and sections — applied to `spec-source/`, the source
of the newest candidate. It runs in a **fresh subagent** context, and the
validation and the audit are separate steps (7 and 8) in fresh subagents
of their own.

Before invoking the prompt, resolve the iteration number `N` as
described in the header (Version numbering): scan
`output/v*-sgai-spec.md`, parse the integer after the leading `v`
in each filename, and set `N = max(found) + 1`. **`output/` is emptied
on promotion, so an empty `output/` does not mean no build has
happened**: read the promoted candidate from `dist/PROVENANCE.md` and
count from there instead. Only when there is neither a candidate in
`output/` nor a `dist/PROVENANCE.md` is `N = 1`. Reusing a number git
history already holds for a different document is the failure this
guards against.

The base `v<B>` is the newest candidate in `output/` (compared numerically
on both components). Preconditions, each stopping the build with its
reason verbatim:

1. `output/` holds a candidate. If it does not, stop: a major is a delta
   on a base, and the source alone does not say which commit of
   `context/` it was built from.
2. `python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --check output/v<B>-sgai-spec.md` exits
   0 (the invariant in the header).
3. `git status --porcelain -- context/` prints nothing.
4. `ANCHOR` is `bin/check-incremental.py anchor v<B>`; exit 2 stops.

Skip rule: if `git diff --quiet ANCHOR..HEAD -- context/` holds (no
delta), no `needs-structure` row is pending (an
`output-analysis/v<X>-context-delta.md` with no `output/v<X>-sgai-spec.md`
and `Context base:` equal to `ANCHOR`) and no `MAJOR_BRIEF` was given →
log `[SKIPPED] apply-context-delta (major) → output/v<B>-sgai-spec.md`, and
from here on `v<N>` means `v<B>`: the post-spec steps and the loop run on
it under their own skip rules.

Otherwise → dispatch a fresh subagent that follows
`prompts/2-build/apply-context-delta.prompt` with `MODE=major`, base
`v<B>`, target `v<N>`, `ANCHOR`, `HEAD` and `MAJOR_BRIEF` if any, and log
`[BUILT] apply-context-delta (major) → output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md`. The step
applies the one-time cleanup of the old pipeline's marks first
(`bin/strip-legacy-annotations.py`, its §1): the first build on the new
pipeline removes them, every later one finds nothing.

Then, before paying for any analysis:

```bash
python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --check output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md
python3 bin/lint-spec.py output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md
python3 bin/check-incremental.py scope v<B> v<N> --max-fraction inf
```

All three exit 0, or the build stops: the first two say the candidate is
the source's and clean, the third that it changed exactly the sections
its trace declares (no cap: a major may change any amount). On a failure
the candidate does not go on: undo the patches (Step 9.7's restore,
applied to every `v<N>-delta-patch-<k>.json` from the highest `k` down,
and delete the files the trace lists as `added` under **Artefacts
changed**), check that `bin/assemble-spec.py --check
output/v<B>-sgai-spec.md` exits 0 again, delete the candidate, keep the
trace and the patches for inspection, and report the outputs verbatim.

`bin/check-incremental.py anchor v<N>` reads `Context head` from
`output-analysis/v<N>-context-delta.md`, so the next incremental starts
from the right commit without the candidate having to be committed
first.

#### Step 6.1 — Emit the extension schema (blocking)

Right after Step 6, run inline (deterministic code, no subagent):

```bash
bin/emit-extension-xsd.py output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md
cmp output/v<N>-sgai-extension.xsd spec-source/syntax/sgai-extension.xsd
```

The §5.10.1 block is `spec-source/syntax/sgai-extension.xsd` included by
the `{{xsd}}` directive, so the emitted file is that file: the script
copies the block byte for byte (ADR 0047) and writes it only if it
compiles together with ISO's 6th-edition `DASH-MPD.xsd`, and `cmp` says
the copy is the source. A `cmp` difference is a runtime error: the
candidate is not the source's.

- **Exit 0** — log `[BUILT] emit-extension-xsd → output/v<N>-sgai-extension.xsd`
  and continue.
- **Exit 1** (the block does not compile, E-1) or **exit 2** (no such
  block, more than one, or ISO's pinned schema unavailable) — **stop
  the build** and report the script's output verbatim. A candidate
  without its schema does not go on to Step 7.
- **Exit 64** — the invocation was wrong; fix the call.

Skip rule: if `output/v<N>-sgai-extension.xsd` exists AND `cmp` with
`spec-source/syntax/sgai-extension.xsd` holds AND `bin/assemble-spec.py
--check output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md` exits 0 → log
`[SKIPPED] emit-extension-xsd → output/v<N>-sgai-extension.xsd`.

### Stage 3 — Post-spec analysis (`3-post-spec/`)

Run after the major (or incremental) delta. The order is the order of
what each step reads: code first, so the LLM steps read its results.

**8.0 → 7 → 7.5 → 8.** The conformance tests run first because the
validation takes its `code` verdicts from them; the detail review reads
the validation's flag ids; the audit reads the tests and the trace of what
changed. The numbers are kept from the old order so references to them
stay valid.

#### Step 8.0 — Conformance tests (deterministic, inline, first)

The conformance verdict is computed by code (ADR 0053), over the source
(ADR 0063), which is the candidate's by the header's invariant. Run
inline — deterministic, so no subagent:

```bash
python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --check output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md
python3 bin/check-dash-conformance.py --source spec-source \
  --out-md output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.md \
  --out-json output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.json
```

The first command must exit 0 (otherwise the source is not the
candidate's and the tests would judge another document: runtime error).
`--source` runs levels 1 and 2 on each fixture by the class
`examples/index.yaml` declares, with the source's extension schema, plus
the source-only rules (FIX-1, FIX-2, C06-1a/b, DASH-REF): see its
`--help`. Its exit is recorded, and **it does not stop the build**: `0`
nothing fails, `1` at least one `FAIL`, `2` cannot tell (ISO's pinned
schema unavailable or mismatched, a level-2 rule that no longer matches
`context/03`), `64` wrong command line. A `FAIL` is an issue for Step 9
and blocks promotion through `bin/check-promotable.py`; a `2` makes that
check exit `2`.

Its green says every rule decidable by code passed on the instances the
source contains. It says nothing about what is `NOT_VERIFIABLE` (listed
with its reason) or about constructs the spec has no example of.

The legacy Players (`--players`, level 3a) are not a build step: they
inform and do not block until their runs are stable (ADR 0057). They are
run by hand, or through the demo (`--demo`), on the assembled document.

- If `output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.json` exists AND the
  `assemble-spec --check` above exits 0 AND the `.json` is newer than every
  file `output-analysis/v<N>-source.sha256` lists → log
  `[SKIPPED] check-dash-conformance → output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.json`.
- Else → run them and log
  `[BUILT] check-dash-conformance → output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.{md,json} (exit <e>)`.

#### Step 7 — Validate spec (code; the model only in single-turn calls)

It runs after Step 7.5, so the validation routes the review's flags (§5 of
the sidecar). It is one command, inline:

```bash
python3 bin/validate.py v<N> --prev v<P> --mode <major|incremental|refine>
```

`bin/validate.py` is `prompts/3-post-spec/validate-spec.prompt` made
code: population, sources, what is walked, batches, merge, trace, sidecar
and summary are deterministic, and the model is used only in single-turn
calls with no tools and no `CLAUDE.md` (the walkers and the open search).
Independence from the agent that applied the delta holds because none of
those calls carries its context. Do not dispatch a subagent for this step:
a subagent's fixed context (~64k tokens per call, measured in phase 10) is
what made the old validation the most expensive step.

The prompt defines it with iteration `N` and its
mode (`major` here; `incremental` from `build-incremental`; `refine` in
Step 9). It writes the sidecar, the trace (`v<N>-trace.json`, checked by
`bin/check-trace.py`) and the machine summary; it reads the units' source
(`code` / `vector-pending` / `llm`) from the conformance tests of Step
8.0. In a major and an incremental it walks every unit that code does not
decide and runs the **open search** — the one read of the whole document
in the pipeline (ADR 0068); in a refine, only the units whose sections
changed (`bin/check-trace.py stale`).

- If the three outputs exist AND the sidecar is newer than
  `output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md` and than
  `output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.json` → log
  `[SKIPPED] validate-spec → output-analysis/v<N>-spec-validation.md`.
- Else → run it and log
  `[BUILT] validate-spec → output-analysis/v<N>-spec-validation.md (+ trace, summary)`.

#### Step 7.5 — Review the names

`prompts/3-post-spec/review-spec-details.prompt` judges only what needs
judgement: an extension name that reuses a base DASH name with different
semantics, or a new name for a base concept (ADR 0069). It reads the list
`bin/name-candidates.py` builds from the extension schema and ISO's
`DASH-MPD.xsd`, and only the sections that list names — never the spec
whole. The other categories the old review covered are code: tables
generated (`bin/gen-syntax-tables.py --check`), cross-references and ids
(`bin/lint-spec.py`), examples included byte for byte and validated (Step
8.0).

**It edits nothing.** A fix it is sure of is written as patch ops in
`output-analysis/v<N>-detail-review.patch.json`, which the next refine
(9.1) includes in its own patch; a name that needs a decision is a flag in
the log. So the candidate validated in Step 7 is the candidate promoted,
byte for byte.

**Execution mode**: deterministic over its inputs; it may run inline.

Before it, run the code half of the old review, inline, and record each
exit in the log line (a non-zero exit is an issue for Step 9, not a stop):

```bash
python3 bin/gen-syntax-tables.py --check
python3 bin/lint-spec.py output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md
```

- Skip rule (owned by the prompt): the log exists AND is newer than all of
  `output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md`, `spec-source/syntax/sgai-extension.xsd`,
  `context/06-naming-and-namespaces.md`, `bin/name-candidates.py` and the
  prompt → log `[SKIPPED] review-spec-details → output-analysis/v<N>-detail-review.md`.
- Else → invoke `prompts/3-post-spec/review-spec-details.prompt` with
  iteration `N` and log
  `[BUILT] review-spec-details → output-analysis/v<N>-detail-review.md (<f> flags, <m> with patch ops)`.

#### Step 8 — Suspicions audit (fresh subagent, once per major or incremental)

The LLM audit is the tests' complement: it reports, as suspicions, places
where it suspects the base standard is violated or ambiguous and no test
decides. It gives no verdict and blocks nothing (ADR 0053, 0057). It runs
**once per major or incremental, over the constructs new or changed**
(ADR 0071) — the sections and artefact files of
`output-analysis/v<N>-context-delta.md` — and **never inside the Step 9
loop**: a refine adds no construct, and a suspicion confirmed by code
becomes a rule of the tests, which the loop does run.

This step MUST dispatch a **fresh subagent**, separate from the agent that
applied the delta and from the validation's. Context overlap biases the
reading.

- If `output-analysis/v<N>-dash-conformance-audit.md` exists AND is newer
  than `output-analysis/v<N>-context-delta.md` and
  `output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.md` → log
  `[SKIPPED] audit-dash-conformance → output-analysis/v<N>-dash-conformance-audit.md`.
- If Step 6 was skipped (no delta, so nothing new or changed) and the
  candidate has an audit → log the same `[SKIPPED]`; with none, there is
  nothing to scope it to: log `[SKIPPED] audit-dash-conformance → no
  delta to audit`.
- Else → dispatch a fresh subagent that follows
  `prompts/3-post-spec/audit-dash-conformance.prompt` with iteration `N`
  and log
  `[BUILT] audit-dash-conformance → output-analysis/v<N>-dash-conformance-audit.md`.

### Stage 4 — Auto-refine (`4-auto-refine/`)

Loop until convergence (or `MAX_REFINEMENTS`, or regression).

#### Step 9 — Auto-refine until convergence

After the major delta (steps 6–8) produces `v<N>-sgai-spec.md`, its
extension schema and its sidecars, the orchestrator enters a
minor-refinement loop that drives the spec toward convergence without
changing requirements. The loop is gated by `BUILD_ALL_AUTO_REFINE`
(default `true`) and capped at `MAX_REFINEMENTS` iterations (default `5`);
both are configurable via `.env.agent` — see "Step 9 configuration" below.

If `BUILD_ALL_AUTO_REFINE=false`, skip the loop entirely and log
`[SKIPPED] auto-refine → BUILD_ALL_AUTO_REFINE=false`. The major build
artefacts (`v<N>-...`) are the final outputs of this run.

A round reads sections, never the document: the refine reads the sections
its issues point at, the validation walks only the units whose sections
changed, and the rest is code. The audit does not run in the loop (ADR
0071).

Otherwise, let `M := 0` (the major spec is the implicit `v<N>.0`
baseline; below, `v<N>.<M>` with `M = 0` names `v<N>`) and iterate
`K = 1` to `MAX_REFINEMENTS`:

  9.1 **Refine, as a patch.** Run `prompts/4-auto-refine/refine-spec.prompt`
      (fresh subagent).
      - Input: `v<N>.<M>`, its source (`spec-source/`, the invariant), its
        sidecars, and `output-analysis/v<N>.<M>-detail-review.patch.json`
        if Step 7.5 wrote one (the refine includes its ops).
      - Output: `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-patch.json` (the patch as
        recorded by `bin/apply-spec-patch.py --record`, with what undoing
        it needs), `spec-source/` patched, and
        `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-refine-gaps.md` when something could not
        be resolved by a minimal edit.
      - Log `[BUILT] refine-spec → output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-patch.json (<X> ops, <S> sections, <F> files)`.

  9.2 **Assemble and check, inline:**

      ```bash
      python3 bin/gen-syntax-tables.py --write      # only if the patch changed syntax/
      python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --version v<N>.<M+1> \
        --out output/v<N>.<M+1>-sgai-spec.md \
        --manifest output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-source.sha256
      python3 bin/lint-spec.py output/v<N>.<M+1>-sgai-spec.md
      ```

      (The refine may already have assembled: then `bin/assemble-spec.py
      --check output/v<N>.<M+1>-sgai-spec.md` exiting 0 replaces the
      second command.) Then Step 6.1 against `v<N>.<M+1>` (emit the schema,
      `cmp` with the source's). An assembly exit 2 or a schema that does
      not compile is a runtime error of the iteration (below).

      **A lint finding is a defect the refine introduced** (the major
      left the document clean, Step 6). Undo the round (the restore of
      9.7), and run 9.1 once more with `lint-spec`'s output added to the
      refine's issue list. A second lint failure undoes the round again
      and ends the loop as `[ERROR] iter K: lint`.

  9.3 **Conformance tests** — Step 8.0 against `v<N>.<M+1>`.
      - Output: `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-conformance-tests.md` and `.json`.

  9.4 **Names** — Step 7.5 against `v<N>.<M+1>` (its code half and the
      names-only review). Its patch, if any, is the next round's input.
      - Output: `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-detail-review.md` and, if any,
        `v<N>.<M+1>-detail-review.patch.json`.

  9.4a **Validation, scoped to what changed** — Step 7 against
      `v<N>.<M+1>` in `refine` mode: `validate-spec` walks the units of
      `bin/check-trace.py stale output-analysis/v<N>.<M>-trace.json --old
      output/v<N>.<M>-sgai-spec.md --new output/v<N>.<M+1>-sgai-spec.md`
      (plus the new and the not-`met` ones, as its §B.2 says), carries
      every other unit over from `v<N>.<M>`, and re-examines the open
      search only on the changed sections.
      - Output: `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-spec-validation.md`, its
        `.summary.json` and `v<N>.<M+1>-trace.json`.

  9.4b **The audit is carried, not re-run.** Copy
      `output-analysis/v<N>.<M>-dash-conformance-audit.md` to
      `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-dash-conformance-audit.md` unchanged
      (`cp`): its header still names the candidate it audited, which is
      true, and the promotion's file set and `check-promotable.py` find
      the sidecar where they look for it. Log
      `[CARRIED] audit-dash-conformance → v<N>.<M+1> (audited v<N>.<M'>)`.

  9.5 Run `bin/check-promotable.py`. This is the loop's **stop
      condition**, and it runs before the comparison because a stop
      makes the comparison unnecessary work.

      It answers one question: **is what remains something the
      pipeline can fix on its own?** Read its exit code, and its
      last line for the counts.

      - **exit 0** — nothing blocks. The candidate is as far as the
        pipeline can take it. Log `[CLEAR] at v<N>.<M+1>, nothing
        blocks`, break the loop, and proceed to Final summary. Do
        NOT promote it into `dist/` — this step, like every other,
        writes no file there.

      - **exit 1 with `NEEDS_HUMAN=0` and `PIPELINE>0`** — what
        blocks is pipeline work, plus possibly `context/` changes a
        written source dictates (`CONTEXT_CHANGE`). Log `[ITER K]
        blocked by <n>, <p> pipeline, <c> context change` and
        **continue the loop**: that is exactly what the loop is for,
        and nobody needs to be told.

      - **exit 1 with `NEEDS_HUMAN=0` and `PIPELINE=0`** — all that
        blocks is `context/` changes a written source dictates
        (validation §5.d). The loop cannot apply them: nothing in it
        edits `context/`. **Break the loop** with outcome
        `CONTEXT_CHANGE` and reproduce the script's `Lo aplica el
        coordinador` group verbatim in the Final summary. Whoever
        invoked this build applies each replacement text, relaunches
        (`build-incremental`), and informs the owner.

      - **exit 1 with `NEEDS_HUMAN>0`** — run 9.5a first, then read
        the check again and branch on its new result — without
        running 9.5a a second time. If `NEEDS_HUMAN>0` still holds
        but `PIPELINE>0` too, **continue the loop**: the pipeline's
        items get fixed while the person's items wait, carried
        unchanged into each new sidecar, and are not re-checked by 9.5a
        again. Only when `NEEDS_HUMAN>0` and `PIPELINE=0`, **break the
        loop** and reproduce the script's
        `Necesita a the project lead` group verbatim in the Final summary,
        together with its `Lo aplica el coordinador` group if it has
        one. That short list is the reason this check exists: what
        reaches a person is what a person has to resolve, and
        nothing else.

      - **exit 64** — the invocation was wrong, not the build. The
        step fails and the loop breaks: nothing was measured, so
        there is nothing to carry on from. Fix the call.

      - **exit 2** — cannot tell. **Do not continue**, and do not
        treat it as a stop either: re-run
        `prompts/3-post-spec/validate-spec.prompt` against
        `v<N>.<M+1>` once, then run the check again. If it exits 2 a
        second time, break the loop and surface the script's reasons
        verbatim. A walk that cannot be trusted is not a build that
        can be judged, and carrying on would let a truncated walk
        read as a clean one — which is the failure this whole step
        exists to make impossible.

  9.5a **Re-check before stopping.** Dispatch a **fresh subagent** —
      it did not write the validation, so it is not defending its
      routing — over `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-spec-validation.md`
      and the `Necesita a the project lead` list of 9.5. For each item, it
      runs the resolution chain of
      `prompts/3-post-spec/validate-spec.prompt` §5: `context/`
      (R1.5, the DP principles, the carrier conventions of
      `context/06-naming-and-namespaces.md`), then
      `.project/decisions/`, then the base standard (clause and
      quoted sentence, per "Grounding against the standard" in
      `CLAUDE.md`). An item a source answers is moved, in the
      sidecar itself, out of its §5.b / §5.c table and into:
      - **§5.a** when the fix leaves `context/` untouched — a row
        with its concrete edit and the source as Citation, into the
        overflow table if the five-row cap is full;
      - **§5.d** when the fix is a `context/` edit the source
        dictates — a row with the replacement text and the source.
      It also sets the item's `Disposition` in the coverage map, and
      the heading counts, to the new bucket, and deletes the old row:
      `bin/check-promotable.py` reads the bucket from the §5 tables,
      so a row left in two tables is routed by whichever comes last.
      An item no source answers stays where it is, with its
      recommendation. Log `[RECHECK] <m> of <n> answered by a source:
      <a> to 5.a, <d> to 5.d`, then re-run `bin/check-promotable.py`.

  9.6 Run `python3 bin/compare-candidates.py v<N>.<M+1>` (ADR 0070).
      The baseline is whatever is published in `dist/`, not `v<N>.<M>`.
      - Output: `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-comparison.md`; the verdict
        is the first word(s) of its stdout line and the `## Verdict` of
        the file. Exit 2 means the two sides are not comparable (or a
        candidate sidecar is missing): treat it as no verdict, keep
        `v<N>.<M+1>`, log `[NO VERDICT] <its reason>`, break the loop.
      - If `dist/` holds no sidecars there is no baseline: the step
        emits no verdict. Keep `v<N>.<M+1>` as the head, log
        `[NO BASELINE] nothing published in dist/, kept
        v<N>.<M+1>`, break the loop and proceed to Final summary.
        Do NOT fall back to comparing against `v<N>.<M>` — that is
        the measurement this build model removed.

  9.7 Read the verdict line from the comparison file:

      - **BETTER THAN PUBLISHED** (the rule of
        `bin/compare-candidates.py`, see "Step 9 configuration"):
          keep `v<N>.<M+1>`, its extension schema, its sidecars
          and its comparison.
          Log `[ITER K] BETTER THAN PUBLISHED, <X> issues below
          dist/, <Y> remaining`.
          Set `M := M+1`, continue the loop.

      - **NO BETTER THAN PUBLISHED** (nothing worse and nothing
        better than the published build):
          keep `v<N>.<M+1>` as the head.
          Log `[NO BETTER] at v<N>.<M+1> after K iterations`.
          Break the loop and proceed to Final summary.

      - **WORSE THAN PUBLISHED** (a unit, the conformance `FAIL` or
        the blocking routed findings got worse than the published build):
          rollback — **the source first, then the files** (the restore
          below, which 9.2 and the error handling also use):

          ```bash
          python3 bin/apply-spec-patch.py --reverse output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-patch.json
          python3 bin/gen-syntax-tables.py --write    # only if the record's files_changed has syntax/
          python3 bin/assemble-spec.py --check output/v<N>.<M>-sgai-spec.md
          ```

          `--reverse` rebuilds the source from the record: it refuses,
          writing nothing, when `spec-source/` moved since the patch was
          applied (its sha256 is not the record's after) or when what it
          rebuilds is not the source before (not the record's sha256
          before). The last command must exit 0: `spec-source/` is again,
          byte for byte, the source of `v<N>.<M>`. A refusal or a
          non-zero exit is an `[ERROR]` (below) with the outputs verbatim,
          and nothing is deleted: the operator has to see the state.

          Then delete what the iteration produced:
            `output/v<N>.<M+1>-sgai-spec.md`,
            `output/v<N>.<M+1>-sgai-extension.xsd`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-patch.json`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-source.sha256`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-refine-gaps.md` (if any),
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-conformance-tests.md`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-conformance-tests.json`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-spec-validation.md`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-spec-validation.summary.json`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-trace.json`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-detail-review.md`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-detail-review.patch.json` (if any),
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-dash-conformance-audit.md`,
            `output-analysis/v<N>.<M+1>-comparison.md`.
          Log `[WORSE] at iter K, <N> net issues above dist/,
          kept v<N>.<M> as final`.
          Break the loop and proceed to Final summary.

          Why the record and not git: the pipeline commits nothing
          (commits are the operator's), so there is no commit of
          `v<N>.<M>`'s source to return to, and stashing would sweep up
          whatever else the tree holds. The record already carries the
          state before and both sha256, so the undo is checked at both
          ends by the script and a third time, independently, by the
          assembly check against the previous candidate.

      Accepting a minor here means the loop keeps building on it.
      **No verdict promotes anything into `dist/`.** Step 9 never
      writes there, in any branch, including the one where the
      candidate beats the published build by a wide margin.
      Promotion is a decision taken outside this pipeline, against
      the criterion in `CLAUDE.md` (the `dist/` entry of "Layout").

  9.8 If `K == MAX_REFINEMENTS` was reached without a
      NO BETTER / WORSE exit:
          log `[MAX_ITER] cap reached at v<N>.<M>, last successful`.
          Break the loop and proceed to Final summary.

On runtime errors during any sub-step (model failure, timeout,
sidecar generation exception): break the loop, keep the last
successful `v<N>.<M>`, log
`[ERROR] iter K: <one-line reason>, kept v<N>.<M> as final` and
proceed to Final summary. Do NOT delete partial artefacts on error —
surface them so the operator can inspect. One thing is restored: if the
round's patch was applied and `output/v<N>.<M+1>-sgai-spec.md` was not
assembled, run the restore of 9.7 (its three commands), so that
`spec-source/` is again the source of the newest candidate in `output/`
(the header's invariant). When the candidate was assembled, the source is
already its source and nothing is undone.

##### Step 9 configuration

- `MAX_REFINEMENTS` (default `5`): maximum number of iterations of
  the auto-refine loop. Configurable via `.env.agent`.
- `BUILD_ALL_AUTO_REFINE` (default `true`): set to `false` to skip
  Step 9 entirely. Use this for validation-only major builds where
  the operator wants to inspect `v<N>` before committing to the
  refine loop.
- **The verdict is the rule written in `bin/compare-candidates.py`**
  (`--help`): unit by unit on the units both sides walked, WORSE when a
  unit clear in `dist/` is blocking in the candidate, or conformance
  `FAIL` or blocking routed findings rise; BETTER when not worse and
  something improved; NO BETTER otherwise. The audit's suspicions are
  reported and never enter it.

### Final summary

Print a summary block:

```
SGAI build summary (iteration v<N>, head v<N>.<M_final>, YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM):
  [OK|FAIL]       gates 0, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] analyze-dash-gap                → context-analysis/dash-gap-analysis.md
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] build-error-semantics           → context-analysis/error-semantics.md
  [BUILT]         analyze-iab-ad-templates        → context-analysis/iab-ad-templates.md
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] legacy cleanup                  → output-analysis/v<N>-delta-patch-0.json (<c> comments, <g> gaps sections, <s> status lines) | none left
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] apply-context-delta (major)     → output/v<N>-sgai-spec.md (base v<B>; <p> placed, <u> not placed; <S> sections, <F> artefact files; lint 0; scope IN_SCOPE)
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] emit-extension-xsd              → output/v<N>-sgai-extension.xsd (= spec-source/syntax/sgai-extension.xsd)
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] check-dash-conformance          → output-analysis/v<N>-conformance-tests.{md,json} (--source; exit <e>; FAIL <f>, NOT_VERIFIABLE <nv>)
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] validate-spec                   → output-analysis/v<N>-spec-validation.md (+ trace, summary; check-trace exit 0)
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] review-spec-details             → output-analysis/v<N>-detail-review.md (<f> flags, <m> with patch ops; gen-syntax-tables --check <e1>, lint <e2>)
  [BUILT|SKIPPED] audit-dash-conformance          → output-analysis/v<N>-dash-conformance-audit.md (<s> suspicions, over <S> sections and <F> files)
  [SKIPPED|CLEAR|NEEDS_HUMAN|CONTEXT_CHANGE|UNCERTAIN|NO_BETTER|WORSE|NO_BASELINE|MAX_ITER|ERROR] auto-refine → head=v<N>.<M_final> after <K> iterations (<R> rolled back, source restored: assemble --check v<N>.<M_final> exit 0)
```

The auto-refine line summarises the Step 9 outcome:
- `SKIPPED` — `BUILD_ALL_AUTO_REFINE=false`, no iterations ran.
- `CLEAR` — `check-promotable.py` found nothing blocking. The
  candidate is as far as the pipeline takes it; promotion is a
  separate, human decision.
- `NEEDS_HUMAN` — the loop stopped because something needs a decision
  it cannot take, and the re-check of 9.5a found no source answering
  it. **The short list goes here, verbatim**, one line per item: it is
  the whole output of the run for whoever reads it.
- `CONTEXT_CHANGE` — all that blocks is `context/` changes a written
  source dictates. The `Lo aplica el coordinador` group goes here
  verbatim; the invoker applies it, relaunches and informs the owner.
- `UNCERTAIN` — `check-promotable.py` exited 2 twice. The reasons go
  here verbatim; the head is the last built iteration and no verdict
  about it is claimed.
- `NO_BETTER` — a NO BETTER THAN PUBLISHED verdict ended the loop at
  `v<N>.<M_final>`.
- `WORSE` — a WORSE THAN PUBLISHED verdict rolled back; head is the
  last accepted iteration.
- `NO_BASELINE` — `dist/` publishes no sidecars, so no verdict was
  possible; head is the last built iteration.
- `MAX_ITER` — `MAX_REFINEMENTS` cap reached without natural exit.
- `ERROR` — a sub-step failed; head is the last successful
  iteration.

Where `M_final == 0` means the auto-refine never accepted a minor
iteration (either skipped, regressed on the first attempt, or
errored before the first acceptance) and the major spec is the
final head.

Language: English.
